===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{alternative case form of|en|rubicon||a [[limit#Noun|limit]] that when [[exceed]]ed, or an [[action]] that when [[take#Verb|taken]], cannot be [[reverse#Verb|reversed]]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[Thomas Paine]|authorlink=Thomas Paine|chapter=Preface|title=Prospects on the Rubicon: Or, An Investigation into the Causes and Consequences of the Politics to be Agitated at the Meeting of Parliament|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:John Debrett|J[ohn] Debrett]],{{nb...|Piccadilly.}}|year=1787|page=iii|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PN9bAAAAQAAJ&pg=PP3|oclc=1102700980|passage=Fortunately for England ſhe is yet on the peaceable ſide of the '''Rubicon'''; but as the flames once kindled are not alway eaſily extinguiſhed, the hopes of peace are not ſo clear as before the late myſterious diſpute began.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=James M. Mathews|authorlink=James M. Mathews|title=Sermon CCVIII. Critical Periods in the Sinner’s Life.|editor=Austin Dickinson|magazine=The American National Preacher: Or Original Monthly Sermons from Living Ministers of the United States|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Printed by West & Trow|month=January|year=1836|volume=10|issue=8 (number 116 overall)|page=316|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Z5EeAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA316|oclc=914502602|passage=But, my hearers, there are '''Rubicons''' to be passed in our religious and moral course, as well as in our temporal—occasions in the experience of our hearts, which extend their influence so far into the future, that it mainly depends on the decision we ''then'' make, and the purposes we ''then'' form, whether we shall at last be saved or lost for ever.}}
#* {{RQ:Browning Casa Guidi Windows|stanza=V|page=90|passage=Forgive me, ghosts of patriots,— [...] / [...] —for being taught in vain / That while the illegitimate Cæsars show / Of meaner stature than the first full strain, / (Confessed incompetent to conquer Gaul) / They swoon as feebly and cross '''Rubicons''' / As rashly as any [[w:Julius Caesar|Julius]] of them all.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Used-up Classical Allusions|magazine=The British Journal: A Home, Colonial, and General Magazine|location=London|publisher=John Mortimer, publisher,{{nb...|141, Strand.}}|month=January–June|year=1853|volume=III|page=322|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DWQEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA322|oclc=41337867|passage=We are always passing the '''Rubicon''', or being called upon to see somebody else pass it. Considering how often it has been passed, the Rubicon ought to be as well bridged as the Thames. [...] Looking back a few years, we find that that heaven-born minister, Pitt [''i.e.'', {{w|William Pitt the Younger}}], crossed the '''Rubicon''' time after time; and while he was crossing it, [[w:Napoleon|[Napoleon] Buonaparte]] was constantly crossing it also. Later, our [[w:Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington|Wellington]] crossed the '''Rubicon''' when he marched against the French in the Peninsula.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=‘On the Wrong Side of the Rubicon.’: (A Groan from a Mild M.P., Half in and Half out of the Cave.)|magazine=[[w:Punch (magazine)|Punch, or The London Charivari]]|location=London|publisher=Published at the office, 85, {{w|Fleet Street}},{{nb...|and sold by all booksellers.}}|date=16 June 1866|volume=L|page=251|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jhtcAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA251|column=2|oclc=732224722|passage=There's only one thing to be done, / For Ministers to save their bacon; / That's to re-cross the '''Rubicon''', / To ground they ne'er should have forsaken. / [...] / On the wrong side [of] the '''Rubicon''', / Hang me if longer I remain; / [[w:William Ewart Gladstone|{{smallcaps|Gladstone}}]] must just re-build his boats, / And take us back again!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=T[homas] Lawrason Riggs|authorlink=T. Lawrason Riggs|title=On Getting Up|editors=J. Howland Auchincloss [''et al.'']|magazine=[[w:Yale Literary Magazine|The Yale Literary Magazine:{{nb...|Conducted by the Students of Yale University.}}]]|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher=Published by the editors{{nb...|On sale at the Coōperative Store.}}; the Tuttle, Moorehouse & Taylor Company|month=February|year=1908|volume=LXXIII|issue=V (number 650 overall)|page=226|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=JRQBAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA226|issn=0044-0108|oclc=977340881|passage=When one is snugly ensconced under several thicknesses of eiderdown, with the frozen water-bottle sending a cracked and mocking leer from the window sill, getting up is the one thing really irrevocable. It becomes the most final of '''Rubicons''', the most suicidal of bridge-burnings, a leap into an abyss of vaguely dreadful activities,—a fantastic world where people stand on their feet and tie neckties.}}

